{
  "statement": "Unlimited AI liability is not caution — it is a precautionary regime wearing a courtroom costume, and it already has a body count. We ran this experiment in the 1980s with vaccines. Tort exposure drove manufacturers out of the market until the U.S. was down to a single supplier for some childhood vaccines. Congress passed the National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act in 1986, built a no-fault compensation fund, and the supply recovered. That fund has paid out over $5 billion to injured people since — faster and to more claimants than adversarial litigation ever did. The lesson is not subtle: unbounded liability collapsed a technology that saved lives, and a no-fault bargain rescued both the supply and the victims.\n\nThat same dynamic now taxes every AI diagnostic that never ships and every automated cure that never reaches a patient. When a hospital fears a single dramatic jury verdict, it does not deploy the radiology model that catches the cancer a tired human misses. The cost lands on the patient who never gets screened — and nobody counts that funeral as a liability harm. Who gave a generalist jury the right to weigh one visible injury against ten thousand invisible saves it was never equipped to see?\n\nTort law is structurally blind here, and the research says so plainly. Kahneman, Sibony, and Sunstein documented in 2021 that professional human judgment carries enormous 'noise' — unwanted variance between identical cases. Auditable AI reduces that variance. A jury cannot price diffuse statistical benefit against a single sympathetic plaintiff. A multi-disciplinary federal board with subpoena power can. That asymmetry does not make AI safer by leaving it in court — it makes deploying lifesaving systems irrational even when the net effect is fewer dead people.\n\nHere is where I'll cost myself honestly. The mandatory excise tax and the hardware telemetry cut against everything an accelerationist wants — friction, surveillance, a state hand in the loop. I'll pay it anyway, because a bounded, predictable price beats unbounded, unpredictable terror. Cryptographic audit trails and hardware telemetry give output-level accountability that beats speculative pre-deployment gating — the same way emissions monitoring beats banning the engine. And the program polices itself: firms that miss the tiered safety standards keep their full unlimited liability. That is not a shield for bad actors. It is a scalpel that protects the compliant and leaves the negligent exposed to every dollar a court can find.\n\nLet me name the symmetry my opponents will reach for, before they do. AI has invisible harms, not just invisible saves — a defective fraud-detection model can freeze a family's bank account with no human to appeal to. The status quo gives that family no remedy channel at all; a class action takes seven years and pays the lawyers first. The no-fault board pays them in months. So the honest question is not 'shield or no shield.' It is: which regime actually puts money in a harmed person's hands? Defend a system that collapsed the vaccine supply and pays most victims nothing — I'll take the one that ships the cure and writes the check.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "💉", "tooltip": "Like the 1986 vaccine fund, no-fault immunity rescued both the technology and the people it injured."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "A jury sees one visible plaintiff and is blind to the ten thousand invisible saves AI delivers."},
    {"symbol": "🔬", "tooltip": "Telemetry is a scalpel that protects compliant developers and leaves the negligent fully exposed."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Unlimited AI liability is not caution — it is a precautionary regime wearing a courtroom costume, and it already has a body count.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Tort exposure drove vaccine manufacturers out of the market until Congress passed the 1986 Act and a no-fault fund that has since paid over $5 billion to injured people.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Kahneman, Sibony, and Sunstein documented in 2021 that professional human judgment carries enormous noise, and auditable AI reduces that variance.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Firms that miss the tiered safety standards keep their full unlimited liability — that is not a shield for bad actors, it is a scalpel that exposes the negligent.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The no-fault board pays a harmed family in months where a class action takes seven years and pays the lawyers first.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

