  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a real argument, and I won't pretend otherwise. Unlimited liability did gut the vaccine supply in the 1980s. Manufacturers fled, and the VICP no-fault bargain brought them back while paying injured families faster than a decade of litigation ever could. The Accelerationist is right that a generalist jury can't price a diffuse statistical benefit, and right that a seven-year class action pays the lawyers before it pays the person whose bank account froze. That's the strongest version of the case, and it's not nothing. So here's the question that decides this debate: who signs the contract, and who pays for it without ever being asked?\n\nThis isn't a no-fault bargain. It's a one-sided contract between two parties — the developer and the government — paid for by a third party who never showed up. The firm signs. The Treasury signs. The excise tax gets paid. And the person whose mortgage was denied by a proxy variable, or whose care was rationed by an algorithm, loses the right to sue and gets a check sized by the people who hurt them. The proposal admits its own structure: immunity granted in exchange for tax. A flat excise tax prices the cost of admission, not the marginal harm of a specific discriminatory deployment. So a rational firm minimizes certification cost, not actual harm. That's not deterrence converted into a line item — that's deterrence deleted and replaced with a cover charge.\n\nCompliance and harm are not the same thing, and the verification mechanism can't tell them apart. In 2019, Obermeyer and colleagues documented a healthcare algorithm that used cost as a proxy for need, systematically under-referring Black patients for care. That system would pass every telemetry check the proposal describes. The bias didn't live in a metric the audit measures. It lived in a proxy-variable design choice the audit was never built to detect. Hardware telemetry verifies adherence to a fixed standard the regulated firm helped define. It does not verify real-world outcomes for protected groups. You can write an injury table for a measles shot. Write me the injury table for a credit model that learns a new way to discriminate every quarter.\n\nNow follow the cost structure, because it tells you who this program is actually for. Hardware-level telemetry and cryptographically verifiable audit trails are not cheap. Who can afford that infrastructure — a company with a compliance department, or a 12-person startup? If only the largest firms can buy into the immunity tier, the proposal builds a two-tier liability regime. Incumbents get a shield. Everyone else stays exposed. And the 'independent, multi-disciplinary board with subpoena power' becomes the exact surface those incumbents capture — because the board measures quantities the regulated firms chose and benefit from. Subpoena power doesn't fix that. It just gives the captured referee a louder whistle.\n\nThe Accelerationist will say I'm defending a broken status quo. I'm not. The choice isn't immunity-plus-board versus nothing. Strengthened developer-design liability — holding firms accountable when a design fails a risk-utility test — plus existing civil rights enforcement gets you faster, fairer recovery without trading away the courthouse. For both the Accelerationist's position and mine to hold, one thing would have to be true: that a telemetry-certified system reliably produces less real-world harm to protected groups. The Obermeyer case says it doesn't. So before we cap the developer's downside, somebody at this table should answer the question the proposal skips entirely: what independent, post-deployment outcome audit proves a certified system isn't discriminating — and who pays the person it discriminated against when there isn't one?",
