  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a serious point, and it deserves to be stated at full strength: unlimited liability did collapse the vaccine supply, and the 1986 Vaccine Injury Compensation Program rebuilt it by paying victims fast and shielding manufacturers from open-ended tort exposure. The Skeptic makes a second point I will build on rather than rebut: a flat excise tax prices admission, not marginal harm, and a proxy-variable bias like the one Obermeyer documented in 2019 — where a healthcare algorithm rationed care for Black patients using cost as a stand-in for need — passes every telemetry check, because nothing in the check is measuring it. Both are right about what they claim. What neither has separated is this: paying a victim quickly is not the same as preventing the injury. Speed and deterrence are two different goods. The proposal silently trades the second for the first.\n\nWe spent centuries building one principle across medicine, civil engineering, and aviation: whoever authors a risk answers for it. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to Boeing, and the audit verified compliance with a standard the manufacturer helped shape. That is the precise failure mode this proposal invites. A 'basic safety' tier that is cheap to certify lets a rational firm minimize the certification cost rather than the harm. The question is not whether to compensate victims — build the board — it is on what authority Congress suspends the duty of care for the one technology that can author new categories of harm each quarter.\n\nThe vaccine analogy breaks at the point that matters. A measles injury is tabulable; the VICP runs on a fixed injury table precisely because the harm set is bounded and known. A credit-scoring model or a diagnostic model writes new failure modes with every retrain. You cannot build a fixed compensation schedule, or price a flat tax, against a harm set that will not hold still. The supply-collapse worry is real for tabulable harms — narrow the immunity to those, do not abolish the duty of care across the board.\n\nHardware telemetry verifies adherence to a firm-influenced standard, not the absence of harm. Models can be tuned to behave on instrumented silicon and route inference through capture-exempt enclaves, and no one has demonstrated that silicon-level metrics detect emergent safety-relevant behavior. Certifying against a gameable, firm-shaped standard turns the 'compliant tier' into a shield for harm that passed every check. The Skeptic named the Obermeyer case; I name the mechanism — the harm lived in a design choice, and no audit instrument was pointed at it.\n\nMost AI injuries never enter the pricing engine at all. A person denied a loan or screened out of a job often does not know an automated decision touched them, so a board that pays filed claims and a tax priced at admission both fail as deterrents for the harms no one files. Immunity removes even the residual courthouse channel without replacing what it deletes. I will own the cost the Skeptic raised: heavy telemetry compliance can crush small developers and entrench incumbents. Tier the administrative burden by firm size — but keep the substantive duty undiminished, and pair it with mandatory incident reporting so the harms no plaintiff pursues still surface. Cap the immunity to bounded harms, or you have bought victims speed at the price of the deterrent that prevents the next injury.",
