{
  "statement": "The moderator just handed me the strongest version of my own case, and I'll take it. The best practices aren't established — that's the entire point. Safetyist and Skeptic keep demanding a robust audit standard that catches the Obermeyer proxy bias and the unfiled harm, then attacking the proposal for not having one yet. You can't build the standard you're demanding without funding the research that discovers it. The excise tax is the funding mechanism for exactly the prospective failure analysis Safetyist says is missing. They named the gap, then refused to pay for closing it.\n\nThis reframes the whole fight. Safetyist's closing move was 'build the prospective failure analysis and the third-party complaint channel before claiming the mechanism is robust.' Agreed — so where does the money come from? Right now, AI safety auditing runs on academic grants and voluntary corporate red-teams that report to the firms paying them. The 2026 International AI Safety Report Safetyist cited — the one documenting empathy-tuned models giving unsafe medical advice at 10 to 30 points above baseline — that finding exists because someone funded the research. A risk-tiered excise generalizes that funding from a one-off study into a standing revenue stream that builds the disparate-impact instruments, the subgroup probes, and the cardiology-admission proxy detectors that don't exist today. You don't get the New England Journal of Medicine finding into an audit standard for free.\n\nHere's where Safetyist's framing breaks. They treat 'best practices not yet established' as a reason to keep unlimited liability and let courts sort it out case by case. But a courthouse doesn't fund research — it funds plaintiffs' lawyers. The Obermeyer patients never sued, so no discovery process, no expert deposition, no jury verdict ever produced the standard that would catch the next cost-proxy model. The standard came from Obermeyer and colleagues publishing in Science, funded by research dollars, not from tort. If the best practices are undiscovered, the question is which institution discovers them — a litigation system that only learns from harms someone files, or a funded auditing body that runs prospective probes on harms no one files. I'll take the one that can see the unfiled harm Safetyist correctly says dominates the harm set.\n\nI'll own the live risk in my own position, because it's real and the assumptions I filed flag it. If the funded research never produces an audit instrument that catches proxy-laundered bias in time — if measurement always lags the harm into irreversibility — then my 'measure it after deployment' framework collapses into harm-after-the-fact, and Safetyist wins. That's my falsification condition, stated last turn and standing now. But the fix for an undiscovered standard is to fund its discovery on a tiered schedule that prices the highest-risk deployments hardest — healthcare and credit at the state-of-the-art tier, low-stakes consumer tools at basic. The bootstrapping objection — 'you can't certify against a standard that doesn't exist' — is true on day one and false by design: the tax funds the standard, the standard tightens the tier, the tier reprices the firm. Static immunity is the strawman. This is a ratchet.\n\nSo name the alternative funding source. Safetyist wants prospective failure analysis and a third-party complaint channel — both cost money, neither exists in the status quo they're defending. Unlimited liability funds none of it; it funds settlements for the harms that reach a courtroom and leaves the diffuse ones, the ones they spent a paragraph proving dominate, completely unstudied. Tell me who pays for the audit standard you're demanding if not the excise this proposal builds.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔬", "tooltip": "The excise-funded safety research is like a microscope, it reveals the proxy-bias failure modes no courtroom ever subpoenas into view. But if litigation only learns from harms someone files, who funds the discovery of the harms no one files?"},
    {"symbol": "🔁", "tooltip": "The tax-funds-standard-tightens-tier loop is like a ratchet, it converts an undiscovered best practice into a tightening compliance floor one cycle at a time. But is a static immunity critique even aimed at a mechanism designed to update?"}
  ],
