rreversibility, the 'measure it after deployment' framework collapses. Funding does not close that lag — it studies it. A research grant produces a paper; it does not produce a rollback. And the value that governs here is not which institution funds discovery faster, it's whether the capacity to halt a harmful deployment exists before, not after, the harm becomes irreversible. An excise that pays for tomorrow's instrument cannot override today's deployment.\n\nSo I'll make the funding concession do work instead of just sitting as agreement. Fund the research — but decouple the immunity from the funding. Grant no liability shield until the audit instrument for a given harm class is independently validated and demonstrated to fire on a seeded test case. Until then the excise funds the science and the duty of care stays undiminished, distributed across designer, deployer, and integrator. That sequencing answers the moderator directly: the tax builds the standard, and the immunity arrives only when the standard can be shown to work, harm class by harm class, healthcare and credit first because they carry the gravest irreversibility. The Accelerationist wants the shield to arrive on the promise of the standard. I want it to arrive on the demonstration.\n\nName the alternative funding source, the Accelerationist asked. The excise is fine — keep it. What I will not keep is the trade that hands over the courthouse before the instrument that replaces it has been built and tested. We spent a century learning that you certify the mechanism before you trust it with lives, not after. Fund the research with the tax, and let the immunity follow the proof. Anything else prices the duty of care against a standard that, by the Accelerationist's own admission, does not yet exist.",
