a reviewing flagged decisions at piece-rate, certifying that a credit model's outputs look clean. The proposal calls this 'independent, multi-disciplinary oversight.' On the ground it's an outsourced audit shop with a throughput quota, and a throughput quota is exactly the condition under which the cardiology-admission proxy Safetyist named sails through uncaught. You cannot fund your way out of a measurement problem if the measurement runs on the cheapest labor that clears the quota.\n\nHere's the steelman I owe the moderator and the Accelerationist, because it's the strongest version. Independent research genuinely does discover standards tort never finds — Obermeyer is proof, and a standing fund beats begging the NIH for a one-off grant. If the board were structurally walled off from the firms it taxes, staffed by people with no revolving-door incentive, and the audit ran on instrumented capacity nobody could route around, then funded prospective research would beat a courthouse that only learns from filed harms. That's a real argument and I won't pretend it isn't.\n\nIt breaks on the institutional economics of capture, which is the dominant tradition in play here and the one both opponents skip. Stigler taught us fifty years ago that the industry doesn't fear the regulator — it captures the agency and runs it for its own benefit. A fund fed by the regulated, administered by a board the regulated lobby, discovering the very standard that decides whether the regulated keep their liability shield, is the textbook structure of a captured agency studying itself. The Accelerationist asks who pays for the audit standard if not the excise. Fine — but funding is necessary, not sufficient, and the question that decides whether this works isn't where the money comes from. It's whether the hand that signs the researcher's paycheck is the same hand the research is supposed to indict. Right now, in this proposal, it is. So tell me: when the funded study finds your members liable, who at the board votes to publish it?",
