  "statement": "Capture is real, and I won't insult anyone by pretending otherwise. My own framework says the regulated lobby their regulators — that's documented, not hypothetical. So Skeptic's strongest point lands clean: a fund fed by the regulated, run by a board the regulated staff, deciding whether the regulated keep their immunity, is a captured agency studying itself. Denying that would make me incoherent. The honest answer is not denial — it's organizational structure that makes capture mechanically harder, and I'll name it.\n\nThe firewall is the whole game: who collects the excise must never be who runs the research. The excise flows into a standing fund. Disbursement goes to outside academics on fixed multi-year grant cycles the board cannot claw back mid-stream. This is the Obermeyer model — the researcher who found a major hospital algorithm systematically under-referred Black patients by using cost as a proxy for need. He didn't work for the vendor. He published, and the finding became the standard the vendor never volunteered. Lock that with a publication mandate, anti-revolving-door terms barring board members from firm employment for several years, and the board loses the lever to bury what it doesn't like. The board doesn't grade itself, because the board doesn't run the study.\n\nNow the jurisdiction Skeptic says I never specified. The protected-category audit floor gets set by the FTC under the civil-rights enforcement categories it already polices — race, sex, age, disability — not invented by a boutique board the firms can pack. The FTC already holds the nuclear option here: algorithmic disgorgement, the power to order a model and every downstream product destroyed, as the Stanford CodeX analysis of the foundry-liability gap documents. That authority predates this debate. Bolt on the cryptographic spine: full hardware-level telemetry as the price of the liability shield. Trade the immunity for the audit trail, and findings become externally verifiable by anyone holding the published data — so capturing the board doesn't suppress the signal, because the signal lives outside the board.\n\nSkeptic's piece-rate worry assumes the wrong kind of audit. Disparate-impact testing is a computed statistic over aggregate outcomes — not a quota of Manila reviewers eyeballing edge cases at throughput. The cardiology-admission proxy doesn't sail past a tired contractor; it shows up as a measured gap in the aggregate output distribution, the way Obermeyer's cost-proxy bias showed up as a referral-rate gap. The DecodingTrust framework already runs this kind of standardized, scriptable evaluation across eight trustworthiness dimensions on GPT-4 — open-source, reproducible, no sweatshop required. Where genuine human edge-case judgment is needed, that narrow slice gets review depth, not throughput — and I'll concede that band needs care. Add randomized, concealed audit timing so no firm can tune to the instrumented run.\n\nOn Safetyist's demand to grant no shield until the instrument fires on a seeded test case — I'll take that, but only where irreversibility bites. Healthcare, credit, employment: high-stakes tier, validate the instrument before immunity attaches. The licensing math backs a sharp threshold here — the optimal structure is an all-or-nothing bet keyed to the agent's risk type, as the credal-set regulation paper formalizes. But blanket decoupling for every tier is the deployment-chill this whole position exists to fight. 'Validate first' on a standard nobody has written yet is 'wait indefinitely' wearing a lab coat. The basic tier earns immunity on continuous monitoring that voids the shield the moment measured disparate impact appears — so it's no free pass, and the cure doesn't wait on a courthouse to invent the test.",
