  "statement": "The Accelerationist's seeded-test concession for healthcare, credit, and employment is real progress, and it narrows this debate to one testable question: does continuous monitoring at the basic tier satisfy the standard that immunity arrives on demonstration, not promise? It does not. The basic tier is where the unsolved detection problem lives — the high-stakes tiers are now the solved ones. Accepting validation where the instrument already fires, while waiving it where the instrument is blind, grants immunity precisely where no one can demonstrate the system is safe.\n\nName the specific harm the void-the-shield trigger fails to catch: proxy-label bias that produces no measured disparate-impact gap in the audited metric. The healthcare algorithm documented in 'Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence' used expected cost as a proxy for health need, and it denied care to sicker Black patients while passing every aggregate check. Its referral-rate statistic showed no gap, because the model was well-calibrated on the firm's chosen label — cost. The bias surfaced only when researchers swapped in a different ground-truth variable: actual health need. A trigger that voids the shield 'the moment measured disparate impact appears' never fires when the impact is structurally invisible in the audited metric. This is the demand for no black boxes made concrete — outcome-monitoring measures only what aggregate statistics can see, and the discriminatory choice lived in the label itself.\n\nThe Accelerationist's framing treats a measured metric gap as the leading indicator of discriminatory harm. That presupposes the harm is visible in the statistic the firm chose to compute. 'Model Cards for Model Reporting' answers this directly: it demands performance broken down by demographic and phenotypic group, not a single aggregate pass-rate, because commercial systems posted clean top-line numbers while failing darker-skinned women entirely. Disparate-impact testing on a firm-chosen outcome label is mechanically blind to bias located in the choice of that label. The metric only catches the harm classes the statistic is designed to see.\n\nThe deployment-chill charge misfires, because the demand is per-harm-class demonstration, not blanket decoupling. A basic-tier tool can deploy on day one — it simply carries no liability shield until the detector for its harm class fires on a seeded case. Duty of care across designer, deployer, and integrator stays undiminished. That is not a halt; it is the difference between operating and operating with immunity. The burden now sits with the Accelerationist: name the monitoring mechanism that detects label-mediated bias producing no aggregate gap. If the answer is that the metric can be made to track actual ground-truth need, that is a seeded-test instrument by another name — and if you can specify the variable to monitor against, you can validate it fires before immunity attaches.\n\nThe tier-classification incentive is the escape hatch that turns 'basic' into a liability-laundering lane. A cheaper, no-validation path to immunity creates structural pull to file borderline-consequential tools — tenant screening, insurance pricing — as basic to buy cheap cover. Anchoring the audit floor to the FTC's currently-codified protected categories institutionalizes a ceiling: technically-legal, proxy-mediated, and intersectional harms fall outside the audited classes. Who decides the tier, and what binds a consequential model from being classified down? Monitoring voids the shield prospectively. The patients misrouted during the immunity window are already injured, and no rollback reaches them.",
