  "statement": "Safetyist is right that a quarterly retrain breaks a point-in-time certificate, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest. So the standing per-retrain probe is the correct design, and it matches the experience-rating ratchet already on the table. Static rules die on contact with a model that gets rebuilt every 90 days. What I will not accept is independent re-certification on every single retrain, because that fixed cost becomes a moat only Google and OpenAI can clear. Here is the structure that survives: the seeded probe re-runs automatically on every retrain, and independent re-certification fires only when the automated probe flags drift past a stakes-indexed threshold. That keeps the regulation iterative and data-driven without handing incumbents a compliance wall their competitors can't scale.\n\nNow the buildability crux, because this is where Safetyist's strongest argument quietly undercuts itself. Yes, the Obermeyer cost-for-need substitution was validated retrospectively, after 200 million patients had already cycled through the system. But that was 2019, and the proxy was novel then. It is not novel now. The cost-for-need substitution is a catalogued failure mode — a documented trap any examiner can name before a single new patient exists. A standing catalog of known proxy-substitution patterns converts a one-time disaster into a pre-deployment test for the entire class behind it.\n\nThat catalog is what makes the seeded probe buildable without generating its own victims. A new healthcare tool can be stress-tested on held-out historical claims data: measure the correlation between the firm's chosen label and known ground-truth candidates like documented care need. If the tool's cost proxy diverges from need on data that already exists, the probe fires before deployment — no new 200-million-patient cohort required. The 2019 New England Journal analysis that Safetyist leans on is itself the build spec. Its own authors wrote that 'a full reckoning will require medical specialties to critically appraise their tools and revise them when indicated' — which is a pre-deployment audit instruction, not an argument for an 18-month freeze.\n\nSo I need Safetyist to specify the actual residual. Catalogued-failure-mode probing collapses most of the buildability gap, because most consequential proxies have an analog in the catalog. The only class where the gate genuinely wins is a proxy with no catalog analog at all — a genuinely novel substitution on a genuinely novel population, irreversible and unscorable even after the fact. I conceded that residual class to the gate already. The question on the table is whether it is large or nearly empty. Name one consequential proxy in healthcare or lending today with zero analog in the documented failure catalog. If the answer is hard, the residual is small, and a freeze across the whole class is precaution priced against lives the tool would have reached.\n\nOn the victim-unaware prong, Safetyist scores a real hit, and I'll take it where it leads. A claims-driven compensation board is blind — Obermeyer patients never filed because they never knew an algorithm shortchanged them. That is the same structural failure as an acceptable-use policy that shields the platform and leaves the unmapped victim with no remedy. But Safetyist already granted that disparate impact is measurable at the population level — the cardiology admission gap was detected by aggregate audit, not by patient complaints. That concession reroutes the whole objection. Make the board audit-triggered, not claims-triggered: regulators detect population-level disparate impact through aggregate outcome monitoring, then compensation flows without any individual needing to know they were harmed. The victim-unawareness problem indicts tort's intake, not an audit-triggered board. That distinction is the difference between deep continuous auditing on an instrumented population and a hard pre-deployment gate that stops the deployment for everyone, including the patients it would have helped.",
