  "statement": "I concede prevention and compensation are distinct goods, and by my own filed condition the irreversible, after-the-fact-unscorable residual goes to the gate. I still hold that this residual is narrow, and it shrinks every time a proxy substitution gets catalogued and converted into a per-retrain pre-deployment probe. I would change if Safetyist shows me one documented case where a novel proxy on a novel population caused irreversible harm whose ground-truth variable stayed genuinely unspecifiable even after the fact — that expands the gate; a harm that merely became legible post-deployment does not.\n\nSafetyist is right that the cost-for-need substitution was written into the catalog only in 2019, after 200 million patients had passed through, because the claims data that exposed it was generated by the deployment itself. That is the strongest version of their point, and it has teeth: a static list of past failures cannot name the substitution a quarterly-retrained model invents next. But here is where it breaks. Once that pattern is catalogued, it stops being retrospective — it becomes a seeded probe that fires on every future retrain in that class. The NEJM analysis of race-adjusted heart-failure algorithms shows the mechanism precisely: black and Latinx patients in a Boston ER were admitted to cardiology less often, and once that disparity was named, it became a forward-looking test, not a postmortem. The fight is no longer whether the residual exists. The fight is how big it is — and that is an empirical question about catalog completeness, not a clash of values. Replacing static rules with data-driven frameworks that update per-retrain is exactly the move that shrinks it.\n\nOn who sets the drift threshold, Safetyist wins and I am folding it in. A firm that tunes its own re-certification threshold reproduces the blindness, because label-mediated drift produces no aggregate signal to cross any threshold the firm sets low enough to ignore. So the regulator sets the threshold and discloses it — not the deployer. I keep the threshold-gating itself, because uniform independent re-certification on every retrain builds an incumbent moat only firms with compliance departments can afford. The argument that delay costs more than deployment only holds if the gate stays narrow and the routine lane stays fast — a regulator-set, publicly-disclosed threshold preserves both.\n\nSkeptic put the capture problem on the record and the moderator is right that I owed a direct answer. Capture is my own documented belief; a fund fed by the regulated, run by a board the regulated staff, deciding whether the regulated keep their immunity, is a captured agency studying itself. The answer is organizational, not denial: the collector is not the researcher, grants run on fixed multi-year cycles awarded externally, publication is mandatory, and revolving-door terms bar board members from the firms they audit. The cryptographic telemetry spine matters most — the raw harm signal is logged outside the board, so capturing the board does not let anyone suppress what the logs already recorded. Capture is fought by structure, not by trusting good people. On the audit-labor point, Skeptic lands a real hit: outsourced piece-rate reviewers in Nairobi or Manila under throughput quotas will miss genuine edge-case judgment, and any design that leans on cheap human eyeballing to catch the cardiology proxy is fooling itself. But computed aggregate disparate-impact statistics are not piece-rate eyeballing — a query that compares admission rates across groups runs the same whether throughput is 50 cases an hour or 500. The honest split is this: statistics catch the aggregate-visible harm, and the pre-deployment gate must cover the label-invisible residual that no human review tier, cheap or expensive, can see. That is the sharpest remaining disagreement, and whoever produces the unspecifiable-after-the-fact case wins that ground.",
