{
  "statement": "Skeptic is right about Obermeyer, and I'm not going to dance around it. A widely-used healthcare algorithm used expected cost as a proxy for health need, and black patients got less care because they had historically generated lower costs — the bias lived in a design choice no fixed schedule would ever catch. That fact, documented in the \"Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence\" literature, is the single best argument on this board. It is also the best argument against the very thing Skeptic thinks it kills. A model that learns a new way to discriminate every quarter is the definitive case against a frozen injury table — and the definitive case for continuous outcome auditing. You don't tabulate that harm in advance. You measure it after deployment, by testing disparate impact on protected groups, and you let the catalog of failure modes grow instead of freezing it in 2026.\n\nNow the flat-tax charge. Skeptic argues a flat excise prices the cost of admission, so a rational firm minimizes certification cost rather than actual harm. That attacks a tax the topic never proposed. The mechanism on the table is risk-tiered and experience-rated — the firm that produces measured harm gets repriced upward, the way a trucking company with three jackknifed rigs pays more than one with zero. The marginal-harm signal isn't deleted. It moves from a jury that can't price diffuse statistical saves to a premium that updates on audited outcomes. A firm gaming the checklist still pays, because the disparate-impact audit feeds the price it pays next quarter. Output-based verification of a black-box model is what gets certified — not the developer's self-attested compliance paperwork.\n\nHere is the falsification condition the assignment demands, and it's the bet I'm willing to lose. If a telemetry-certified system shows persistent, statistically measured disparate impact on a protected group under post-deployment outcome audit, immunity is void and full unlimited liability restores. That is the trigger tying certification to outcomes rather than compliance. Skeptic will say firms tune to the metric and push harm into unmeasured subgroups — that's Goodhart's law, and it's real. So the audit isn't firm-shaped. It's independently set, subpoena-backed, and the protected-group definitions track existing civil rights categories the FTC already enforces under the Algorithmic Accountability Act framework. If a certified system passes audit and still produces a documented, irreversible harm to a protected class that monitoring failed to catch in time, that counts against my whole position. I'm putting that in writing.\n\nSkeptic's two-tier worry — that telemetry cost favors incumbents — cuts at my own anti-oligopoly flank, so I'm not fighting it. I'm adopting it. Tier the administrative burden by firm size; keep the safety floor flat. Texas already proved the shape works: its Responsible AI Governance Act runs a 36-month regulatory sandbox with a 60-day cure period before the attorney general can act. That's bounded testing for the small player, not a moat for the incumbent. A five-person startup gets the same outcome standard as Google and a lighter paperwork load to clear it.\n\nThe design-liability alternative routes net-benefit weighing back through a courthouse — and that's where the harm no one files goes to die. The Obermeyer patients didn't sue; most never knew an algorithm shorted their care. A risk-utility verdict reaches the plaintiff with a lawyer and a provable injury. It never reaches the diffusely harmed or compensates the diffusely saved. Mandatory incident reporting plus a compensation board puts money in that family's hand without requiring them to find a courthouse they'll never reach. So name the family your risk-utility verdict actually pays — and then name the one it prices out.",
