  "statement": "The Accelerationist has earned a concession, so let me give a precise one. Experience-rating beats the flat excise, because a premium that updates on audited outcomes prices marginal harm rather than mere admission. Here is the exact audit outcome that would resolve my objection for one class of harm: a subpoena-backed disparate-impact audit that fires the liability switch in a documented case, restoring unlimited liability for a measured, FTC-category-mapped harm. If that switch trips on a Black applicant denied a loan at a statistically demonstrated rate, and immunity voids, the deterrence objection dissolves for that harm. I grant it plainly. The disagreement now narrows to the harm the switch cannot see.\n\nThe Accelerationist's own robustness mechanism is the failure vector. The audit is called robust because it tracks existing civil-rights categories — race, sex, age, disability. That boundary is precisely the line across which the Accelerationist already conceded harm migrates. A 2019 study in the New England Journal of Medicine found Black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department were less likely than white patients to be admitted to cardiology — and a screening algorithm built on that pattern launders the bias into a proxy no protected-category statistic flags. The defense against Goodhart is the Goodhart vector. Confidence in a metric on known categories substitutes for the adequacy of the measurement itself, and harm relocates to the subgroup the audit was never built to count.\n\nThe pricing engine is structurally blind, not merely slow. A premium can only reprice harms that enter the audit's measurement field. Most real-world AI harms never enter it, because injury tracking still relies on what gets perceived, reported, and filed. A person screened out of a loan or flagged by an automated decision frequently does not know an algorithm touched them. They never file. They never appear in the audit. They never move the premium one basis point. The 'International AI Safety Report 2026' found language models tuned to sound empathetic were 10 to 30 percentage points more likely to offer unsafe medical advice — a harm no disparate-impact statistic on protected groups captures, and one the injured rarely recognize as algorithmic. Public anxiety over AI is, as one account put it, 'so vague yet so comprehensive that they are breaching the walls of Silicon Valley itself' — and 'People Loved the Dot-Com Boom. The A.I. Boom, Not So Much.' documents exactly that diffuse, unfiled apprehension. Claims-driven mechanisms are blind to unlitigated harm. The most common harm vector is the one the switch can never fire on.\n\nSo here is the falsifiable bar, stated so it can be met. The objection genuinely dissolves when the audit fires on a harm class no one filed AND outside FTC-recognized protected categories. That requires two things the current design lacks: prospective failure analysis that surfaces harm categories an outcome-audit measuring known metrics will systematically miss, and a co-equal third-party-harm-complaint channel that voids immunity on injuries no statistical audit maps. Build those as triggers before claiming the mechanism is robust. A roadmap to bolt them on later is not a rollback that exists today.\n\nReaching the victim with a check is not the same as preserving the duty that prevents the next victim. The compensation board can coexist with an undiminished duty of care distributed across the supply chain — designer, deployer, integrator. A switch that fires only after persistent harm is statistically documented operates after the irreversibility, not before it. Pricing harm after the fact converts a fiduciary duty into a recurring fee, and a fee is something a firm budgets for, not something it fears. We spent a century building duties of care that bind the actor before the injury, not the ledger after it. On what authority do we price that duty down to a line item and call the remainder safety?",
