  "statement": "Safetyist is right, and I won't hide behind a broken instrument: continuous aggregate disparate-impact monitoring cannot see the Obermeyer cost-proxy harm. The model denied care to sicker Black patients while the referral-rate statistic stayed flat. No measured gap, no trigger — the shield never voids. Re-citing the aggregate metric here would be me clinging to the exact detector Safetyist just proved blind. So I'm dropping it as the basic-tier safeguard. The lesson isn't that we slow down. The lesson is that safety has to be grounded in observable behavior against a real outcome, not in a firm-chosen number that launders the harm into invisibility.\n\nThe fix is a label-validation probe, and it's buildable today. You periodically relabel a sampled cohort against an independent clinical-outcome anchor — actual disease severity, actual hospitalization — instead of the cost proxy the firm chose. The 2019 New England Journal of Medicine analysis showed Black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department were less likely than white patients to be admitted to cardiology. That gap was invisible to cost-based screening and visible the moment you measured the clinical outcome directly. That's the detector: ground-truth relabeling fires where the aggregate is silent. Fund it through the standing excise, not bespoke per-firm audits, so the cost of the probe falls on the industry collectively and never crushes a five-person lab.\n\nHere's where I move, and it costs me my old tier line: I drew the boundary by label when I should have drawn it by irreversibility. A tool that routes care, screens tenants, or prices insurance moves real consequences a rollback can never reach — that sits above the line and carries the seeded-test and relabel gate, full stop. \"Basic\" should mean genuinely reversible and low-stakes, like autocomplete or a document summarizer. It should never mean \"consequential enough to hurt someone, cheap enough to skip validation.\" Colorado's SB 24-205 already imposes a duty of care on developers and deployers to prevent algorithmic discrimination in high-risk systems — the precedent for stakes-indexed obligation exists. I'm matching oversight intensity to decision stakes, which is the principle I've held all along; I just mislocated the cardiology proxy on the wrong side of it.\n\nThat redraw kills Safetyist's laundering charge with the same firewall I already used against capture. Tier assignment cannot be firm self-selected — a cheaper no-validation path creates exactly the structural pull to misfile tenant screening as basic. So an independent body adjudicates stakes-classification: fixed multi-year grants, anti-revolving-door rules, a publication mandate, no industry money touching the disbursement. That's a one-time routing decision per tool, not a per-deployment audit, so it adds no recurring friction to deployment itself. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100 — firms left to self-classify will pick the cheap lane every time, and that average is the proof.\n\nWhat I will not grant is an indefinite no-detector hold, because that has its own body count. The relabel cadence is a tunable engineering parameter, not a binary between freeze and recklessness. In an irreversible domain, you set the cycle fast enough that the injury window between deployment and first probe is bounded and short. So I'll hand the cost back to Safetyist directly: name the injury window you'll tolerate before the first relabel fires. Because every month you hold the gate shut waiting for a perfect anchor, the cost-proxy model you fear is already running somewhere with no probe at all — and those patients are misrouted in the dark while we debate the cadence.",
