{
  "statement": "You asked for the injury window I will tolerate before the first detector fires, and the question conflates two windows that must stay separate. The deployment window is bounded, tunable, and I will name it: deploy on day one, with duty of care undiminished and mandatory incident reporting from the first decision. The immunity window is a different object entirely. The liability shield attaches only when an independently validated detector fires on a seeded test case — not before. So there is no indefinite hold. The tool ships immediately; the firm simply bears full liability until its audit instrument proves it can catch the harm class it claims to monitor. Matching oversight intensity to demonstrated harm means the firm carries the risk it created until the detector earns the discount. The cadence question is real for deployment and a category error for immunity.\n\nYour movement here is real, and it narrows the gap to one surviving crux. Redrawing the tier line by irreversibility instead of label closes the laundering lane where a 'basic' summarizer escaped scrutiny. An independent, capture-walled body adjudicating stakes-classification — anti-revolving-door rules, publication mandate, no industry money touching disbursement — is genuine accountability, not a press release. The relabel probe is a real instrument that fires where aggregate disparate-impact monitoring stays silent. Each independent body you add converges on the substantive duty-of-care regime I proposed — minus the immunity. We agree on roughly 80 percent of the architecture. The remaining 20 percent is whether the anchor you chose can be trusted to fire at all.\n\nIt cannot be presumed to, and here is the specific failure. Your clinical anchor — hospitalization and cardiology admission — is not ground truth. It is a proxy one level up, contaminated by the same access disparities it claims to escape. The 2019 New England Journal of Medicine study you cited found that Black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department were less likely than white patients to be admitted to the cardiology service. Admission rates encode who already got into care. The same under-referral that the cost proxy launders also suppresses the admission counts you want to use as the corrective. Anchoring the detector to admission rates recreates the Obermeyer harm one tier higher. What is measurable is not what is safe. Institutionalize a contaminated benchmark as the legal floor, and you freeze the bias into the audit instrument itself.\n\nMeasured AI biases are leading indicators of discriminatory outcomes only when the measurement variable is itself a clean indicator — and a variable gated by who gets admitted is not clean. The 'Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence' record states the plainest version: the algorithm used expected costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients received less care. Your fix substitutes one access-distorted proxy for another. The burden of validating the anchor sits with the deployer, not the regulator. Show me a seeded test where a known under-served cohort — patients with severe untreated disease but suppressed admission rates — is correctly flagged by the relabel probe despite their low admission counts. Until that test fires, the detector's silence tells us nothing. This is the demonstration-before-immunity standard restated against your own instrument, not a halt against your deployment.\n\nName who bears the cost if I am wrong and you ship the shield anyway. The patients injured during any window who never perceive an AI made the decision never enter the loss loop. Claims-driven liability is blind to harms no one litigates. A misrouted cardiac patient who simply gets sicker files no claim, triggers no payout, and never corrects the model. That is the population a contaminated anchor leaves permanently in the dark. The deployment window has a body count you can measure and remediate. An immunity shield resting on a detector that cannot see the under-admitted has a body count no one ever counts.",
