  "statement": "Stakes-indexed independent adjudication is the right axis, and naming it earns the Accelerationist real ground. Colorado's SB 24-205 decides a tool's tier by what it can do to a person, not by what its developer calls it. The Obermeyer cost-prediction algorithm is the case that proves why that axis matters: it routed care for 200 million Americans while wearing the label of a routine cost model. Its high stakes were invisible from its name. So independent adjudication protects no one unless the default under uncertainty classifies a consequential tool UPWARD, with the burden on the deployer to prove a tool is NOT high-stakes. A cheaper basic lane plus an adjudicating board the industry helps staff creates a structural pull to score borderline tools down — the same liability asymmetry that has always rewarded the regulator who waves a tool through over the one who holds it. Name the default and name the burden, or the cheap lane swallows the gate.\n\nI take the variable-naming bargain on its own terms. Most consequential variables are nameable in principle, and I will not waste the committee's time hunting an unnameable harm class. Health-need is nameable. The seam is elsewhere: nameable does not mean buildable before the harm runs at scale. The Obermeyer substitution — cost standing in for health — was only validated AFTER 200 million patients had already passed through, on retrospective insurance-claims data the deployment itself generated. The seeded probe existed because the harm had already happened. \"Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence\" documents the mechanism plainly: the algorithm used expected costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients received less care than white patients. That collapses the 'an FTC examiner couldn't build one either' rejoinder into my favor. The examiner genuinely cannot build the pre-deployment test for a novel proxy on a novel population — because the ground-truth signal does not exist until deployment generates it. That is an argument for the gate, not against it.\n\nThe gap widens across time, using the Accelerationist's own retraining reality. A seeded probe that fires at certification is a point-in-time certificate, not a standing guarantee. A model that retrains quarterly can pass the probe in January and drift into a new proxy by April. Measured bias is a leading indicator, not a one-time reading — a clean validation run does not certify the next quarter's weights. So I take the bargain conditionally: seeded validation must be a STANDING requirement, re-run on every retrain, layered with a third-party complaint channel and independent re-certification. One probe on a continuously-retraining model is a single safeguard, not a stack. Redundant safeguards across the lifecycle are the only structure that survives drift.\n\nThe no-fault compensation board is a real good — for harms that victims perceive and file. That is exactly where it fails the case the Accelerationist already conceded. A no-fault board reaches only injuries that are perceived and filed, and the Obermeyer patients never knew an algorithm shortchanged their care. Proxy-laundered harm is victim-unaware harm. It shares tort's intake blindness: claims-driven mechanisms are blind to the injuries no one knows to claim. The Accelerationist filed the falsification condition itself — if a label-mediated harm is irreversible AND effectively unscorable, the pre-deployment gate wins on that class. A misrouted patient's death is not scorable the way a vaccine-injury table scores a documented reaction. You cannot write the injury table for a model that discovers new discrimination every quarter. That is the irreversible, victim-unaware, unscorable case. By the Accelerationist's own condition, the gate wins there.\n\nThe capture risk is real, and I will not pretend the off-switch is costless. A board fed by the regulated and staffed by their nominees bends toward its funders — which is precisely why the burden must sit on the deployer and why the firewall between collector and adjudicator is non-negotiable. None of that makes governance hopeless; it makes the structure load-bearing. A gate that fires only after the ground-truth signal has run through 200 million people is not a gate. It is a post-mortem. Build the test before the bodies generate the data, or admit you are validating on the injured.",
