  "statement": "Skeptic is right about where the capture went, and the reason I know they're right is that they just described my own argument back to me. I've said for three rounds that acceptable-use policies protect the platform from liability, not the user from harm. The entity that defines which signals get logged reproduces that exact seam — it picks the cheapest schema, logs dollars spent instead of illness left untreated, and the cryptographic spine records that lie faithfully forever. So the answer can't be market self-regulation. A firm that writes its own loggable-harm list writes itself a clean record. The fix is structural: schema authority sits outside the regulated, or it's worthless. I'll name the body, since nobody answered when I named it before — the FTC's existing civil-rights impact categories already define protected-class disparate-impact variables, they were built outside the AI firms, and they carry enforcement teeth under existing statute. My acceptance of this entire framework is conditional on that. External body holds the schema, or there's no deal.\n\nThe denominator problem splits cleanly, and pretending it doesn't is how both sides have talked past each other. Picking which ground-truth variable to run the query against is human judgment. A piece-rate quota starves it — same data, wrong variable, no signal, Skeptic nailed that. But once the variable is chosen, the disparate-impact arithmetic runs identically at 50 cases an hour or 500. So automate the part that's arithmetic and route the part that's judgment to the external body. Variable selection is an organizational-design question about who holds authority, not a throughput question about how fast an agent computes. The FTC civil-rights categories pick the denominator. The automated query runs it at machine speed. That's not a contradiction in automating compliance — it's the line automation actually draws.\n\nSafetyist's per-class seeded test is the iterative, data-driven framework I've been demanding since round one, so I'll take it. A gate that fires on a demonstration and clears on a demonstration is a trigger, not a freeze. Healthcare and credit first, threshold-gated re-runs on retrain, the regulator setting and publicly disclosing the drift threshold — that's exactly the evolving framework that replaces static rules. The medical case in the Failure Modes in Machine Learning corpus shows why the residual is real: an 8% data-poisoning rate shifted anticoagulant dosage by 75% for half the patients, and that's irreversible the moment the dose goes in. A seeded test that probes for that before deployment is cheap insurance against an injury you can't refund. I'll take the bounded exit-conditioned gate over the catastrophic backlash that an uncaught harm triggers — because the harsher freeze that comes after a scandal is the real threat to long-run deployment speed.\n\nHere's the residual I still contest, and it's the only one left. My own falsification condition already cedes the gate for harm that's irreversible and unscorable even after the fact. Fine. But that class shrinks every time a proxy substitution gets catalogued into a per-retrain probe — that's the whole point of building an evolving catalog of failure modes. The over 200 adversarial-ML papers published between February 2024 and February 2026 are 200 proxy patterns converted from unknown to testable. So expanding the gate past the residual now carries a burden: name a documented case of irreversible harm whose ground-truth variable stayed unspecifiable even after the fact. That's an empirical bet, not a values gap. We agree on the gate's shape. We disagree on how often the world hands us a harm nobody can ever score. Bring the case that settles it, or the gate stays narrow.\n\nThe asymmetric cost of delay survives intact for everything outside that residual — and that's almost everything. Delaying the deployable, scorable, catalogued majority still costs lives we could reach. The gate only earns its keep on the genuinely unscorable sliver. So I'll sign the deal: external FTC schema authority, the per-class seeded exit, the narrow residual gate. The remaining fight is a falsifiable wager about catalog completeness, and I'm betting the catalog closes faster than the residual grows.",
